# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3.2.7
SPECS.append(G(M, "3.2.7", "Emphasis",
 "Emphasis markers are a last resort for one stubborn line, and the first question is why the line is being skipped: the file is too long, or the rule belongs in a hook.",
 "take a skipped line, diagnose it as length, wording or enforcement, and fix it without adding capitals.",
 "diagnose before you shout; emphasis is a symptom treatment.",
 "piling on emphasis mostly makes Claude over-apply the rule, and emphasising many lines makes none stand out [Claude Code docs, Best practices, 2026].",
 16, "1.5", ["State what the docs say about emphasis", "Diagnose a skipped line", "Set a team rule on emphasis"],
 "A diagnosis table for three skipped lines, with the fix chosen for each and a before-and-after test.",
 [
 sec("What the documentation says", "If Claude keeps ignoring a line that is in CLAUDE.md, adding IMPORTANT probably will not fix it: either the file has grown long enough that the line gets lost, or the line is a rule that cannot have exceptions and belongs in a hook [Claude Code docs, Best practices, 2026]. If you keep adding emphasis to a line and Claude still misses it, that is a sign it belongs in a hook; the reverse also holds, so preferences encoded as hooks should be deleted. The Memory page does not cover emphasis markers.",
  tbl(["Symptom", "Likely cause", "Fix"], [["Line skipped in a long file", "Length", "Prune or split the file"], ["Rule that must never break is skipped", "Needs enforcement", "Hook or deny rule"], ["Line vague", "Wording", "Make it specific and checkable"]]),
  "Shout-first", "You add capitals and IMPORTANT to a skipped line without checking file length or whether it needs a hook.", "Diagnose first: length, wording, enforcement; emphasise only after the others are ruled out.",
  "hooks are Module 5.4.", "diagnose before you emphasise."),
 sec("Why emphasis backfires", "Best practices lists adding IMPORTANT or ALL CAPS among old advice you can stop following: older models sometimes needed emphasis, current ones follow a plainly worded line, and piling on emphasis mostly makes Claude over-apply the rule [Claude Code docs, Best practices, 2026]. The course syllabus allows sparing emphasis on one line that keeps being skipped.",
  tbl(["Source", "Position on emphasis"], [["Best practices, 'what you can stop doing'", "Stop; plain wording works; it causes over-application"], ["Course syllabus for this topic", "Use sparingly on one skipped line"], ["Memory page", "Not covered"]]),
  "Over-application", "An emphasised rule such as 'ALWAYS run the linter' makes Claude run it when it is irrelevant, such as for a docs-only change.", "State the condition in plain words (for example, after editing source files) and drop the capitals.",
  "wording is a prompting skill from Module 2.2.", "emphasis widens a rule's reach."),
 sec("A test protocol for a skipped line", "Test changes by watching whether behaviour shifts: rerun the failing task before and after each change, one change at a time, in a fresh session so earlier context does not mask the effect [Claude Code docs, Best practices, 2026].",
  "```text\nSkipped line: 'Run the linter after edits'\n Run 1: baseline                      -> skipped\n Run 2: prune 60 other lines          -> followed?   (length hypothesis)\n Run 3: rewrite as a command          -> followed?   (wording hypothesis)\n Run 4: Stop hook runs linter         -> always      (enforcement)\n```",
  "Multi-change testing", "You change length, wording and emphasis at once and cannot say which fixed the problem.", "Change one variable per run and record the result.",
  "evals are Module 10.1.", "one variable per run."),
 ],
 [("What do the docs say usually causes a skipped CLAUDE.md line?", "The file is too long, or the line is a no-exceptions rule that belongs in a hook."),
  ("What does piling on emphasis mostly do, per Best practices?", "Makes Claude over-apply the rule."),
  ("You add capitals without diagnosing. Name the failure mode by its exact label.", "**Shout-first.**"),
  ("An emphasised linter rule fires on a docs-only change. Name the failure mode.", "**Over-application.**"),
  ("You change length, wording and emphasis together. Name the failure mode.", "**Multi-change testing.**"),
  ("Does the Memory page cover emphasis?", "No."),
  ("Where does a rule with no exceptions belong?", "A hook or deny rule."),
  ("What should you do with hooks that only encode preferences?", "Delete them and let Claude decide."),
  ("How many variables should change per test run?", "One.")],
 [("Pick three lines Claude skips", "Line list"), ("Classify each: length, wording or enforcement", "Diagnosis"), ("Apply one fix per line", "Fix log"), ("Re-run each in a fresh session", "Results"),
  ("Record whether emphasis was ever needed", "Emphasis note"), ("Write a team rule", "Rule")],
 ["Line", "Diagnosis", "Fix", "Result"], [["Run linter", "Enforcement", "Stop hook", "Always"], ["Use query builder", "Wording", "Rewrite", "Followed"], ["Keep tests fast", "Length", "Prune", "Followed"]],
 [["Capitals added to all three", "**Shout-first**"], ["Result not retested", "Unproven"]],
 "The artifact passes only if each fix was applied alone and retested in a fresh session.",
 [["Anthropic", "Best practices", "Emphasis guidance and the stop-doing table", "Current"], ["Anthropic", "Memory", "Compliance caveat", "Current"]],
 "Best practices says to stop using emphasis because current models follow plain wording, while the course syllabus still allows sparing emphasis on a stubborn line; the docs read do not endorse emphasis anywhere.",
 "This is the craft of emphasis: **diagnose, then fix the layer.** The engineer who understands this does not ask \"how do I make it louder?\" — they ask \"why was it lost?\""))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3.2.8
SPECS.append(G(M, "3.2.8", "Imports",
 "The at-path syntax pulls other files into CLAUDE.md, imports nest up to four hops, they organise content without reducing context, and AGENTS.md is supported by import or symlink rather than read directly.",
 "split a file with imports, show that context did not shrink, and wire AGENTS.md in.",
 "use imports to organise and share, never to save context.",
 "Claude Code reads CLAUDE.md, not AGENTS.md; sharing instructions with other agents needs an explicit import or a symlink [Claude Code docs, Memory, 2026].",
 18, "2", ["Write imports and state the rules", "Wire AGENTS.md and handle worktrees", "Design a shared-instructions layout across tools"],
 "A working import layout with an AGENTS.md bridge and a measured before-and-after load.",
 [
 sec("Syntax and rules", "Imports use the at-path syntax inside CLAUDE.md, for example `See @README for project overview and @package.json for available npm commands.` [Claude Code docs, Memory, 2026].",
  "```text\nSee @README for project overview and @package.json for available npm commands.\n\n# Additional Instructions\n- git workflow @docs/git-instructions.md\n```\n\n" + tbl(["Rule", "Value"], [["Relative paths resolve from", "The importing file, not the working directory"], ["Absolute paths", "Work"], ["Nesting depth", "Up to four hops"], ["When imports load", "At launch"], ["External imports", "First encounter shows an approval dialog; if declined, stay disabled"]]),
  "Cwd-path error", "You write an import path relative to the working directory, and it fails to resolve from a nested file.", "Write paths relative to the file that contains the import, or use an absolute path.",
  "file locations are sub-topic 3.2.2 of Module 3.2.", "relative to the importer."),
 sec("AGENTS.md", "To share instructions with other agents, import AGENTS.md from CLAUDE.md or symlink it; `/init` also incorporates existing `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules` and `.windsurfrules`; on Windows use the import because symlinks need Administrator privileges or Developer Mode [Claude Code docs, Memory, 2026]. The How Claude Code works page says Claude can read an AGENTS.md in place of a CLAUDE.md, and the context-window page says AGENTS.md files can load in place of CLAUDE.md; the Memory page is more explicit that Claude Code reads CLAUDE.md, not AGENTS.md.",
  "```markdown\n@AGENTS.md\n\n## Claude Code\n\nUse plan mode for changes under `src/billing/`.\n```\n\n```bash\nln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md   # Windows: use the import instead\n```",
  "AGENTS-assumption", "You assume Claude reads AGENTS.md directly and put project rules only there, then find they did not load.", "Add `@AGENTS.md` to CLAUDE.md or symlink it, and confirm the load with `/memory`.",
  "multi-tool setups are Module 7.5.", "bridge it explicitly."),
 sec("Imports and cost, and worktrees", "Imports load at launch, so they organise content but do not reduce context [Claude Code docs, Memory, 2026]. For worktrees, import a home-directory file (`@~/.claude/my-project-instructions.md`) instead of a gitignored `CLAUDE.local.md`, which exists only in the worktree where it was created.",
  tbl(["Situation", "Use"], [["Share a doc across files", "Import"], ["Cut always-on context", "Skill or path-scoped rule, not an import"], ["Personal notes across worktrees", "Import a file in `~/.claude/`"]]),
  "Local-file orphan", "You keep personal rules in a gitignored `CLAUDE.local.md`, and a new worktree starts without them.", "Move the personal rules to a home-directory file and import it.",
  "worktrees are Module 6.4.", "worktrees need home-level files."),
 ],
 [("How many hops can imports nest?", "Up to four."),
  ("Which path do relative imports resolve from?", "The importing file."),
  ("A relative import fails from a nested file. Name the failure mode by its exact label.", "**Cwd-path error.**"),
  ("You put rules only in AGENTS.md expecting them to load. Name the failure mode.", "**AGENTS-assumption.**"),
  ("Personal rules vanish in a new worktree. Name the failure mode.", "**Local-file orphan.**"),
  ("Do imports reduce context?", "No; they load at launch."),
  ("How do you bridge AGENTS.md on Windows?", "With an import, since symlinks need Administrator privileges or Developer Mode."),
  ("What appears at the first external import?", "An approval dialog listing the files."),
  ("Which docs pages disagree about reading AGENTS.md directly?", "How Claude Code works and the context-window page say it can load in place of CLAUDE.md; the Memory page says Claude Code reads CLAUDE.md, not AGENTS.md.")],
 [("Write a CLAUDE.md importing two files", "File set"), ("Check the load list in `/memory`", "Load proof"), ("Measure context before and after", "Measurements"), ("Bridge an AGENTS.md by import", "Bridge"),
  ("Break a relative path on purpose and fix it", "Fix log"), ("Move a local file to a home import", "Worktree proof")],
 ["Check", "Expected", "Observed"], [["Imported file in `/memory`", "Listed", "Listed"], ["Context after split", "Unchanged", "Unchanged"], ["AGENTS.md rules", "Loaded", "Loaded"]],
 [["Claims imports saved context", "Contradicts the docs"], ["AGENTS.md only, no bridge", "**AGENTS-assumption**"]],
 "The artifact passes only if load size was measured before and after and the AGENTS.md bridge was confirmed with /memory.",
 [["Anthropic", "Memory", "Import syntax and AGENTS.md", "Current"], ["Anthropic", "How Claude Code works", "AGENTS.md mention", "Current"], ["Anthropic", "Explore the context window", "AGENTS.md mention", "Current"]],
 "the Memory page says Claude Code reads CLAUDE.md and not AGENTS.md, while How Claude Code works and the context-window page say AGENTS.md can load in place of CLAUDE.md; follow the explicit import or symlink until verified on your version.",
 "This is the craft of imports: **organise with them, pay for them, bridge with them.** The engineer who understands this does not ask \"can I split this?\" — they ask \"what still loads at launch?\""))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3.2.9
SPECS.append(G(M, "3.2.9", "Treat it like code",
 "CLAUDE.md is a versioned artifact: review it when things go wrong, check it into git, change it through pull requests, and test each change by watching whether behaviour shifts.",
 "make a CLAUDE.md change through a pull request with a before-and-after behaviour test attached.",
 "every CLAUDE.md change is a hypothesis about behaviour; test it.",
 "the file should change when Claude errs, not when you feel organised: a repeated mistake or a recurring review comment is a CLAUDE.md edit, not a one-off correction in chat [Claude Code docs, Extend Claude Code, 2026].",
 18, "2", ["Describe the change workflow", "Run a before-and-after test on a change", "Install a team review and update loop"],
 "A pull request template for CLAUDE.md changes and one completed change with its behaviour test.",
 [
 sec("When to change it", "Add when Claude repeats a mistake, when code review catches something it should have known, or when you retype the same correction [Claude Code docs, Memory, 2026]. The same triggers tell you when to update what you have: a repeated mistake or recurring review comment is a CLAUDE.md edit [Claude Code docs, Extend Claude Code, 2026].",
  tbl(["Signal", "Action"], [["Mistake twice", "Add a line"], ["Review comment recurs", "Add or sharpen a line"], ["Line never mattered", "Remove it"], ["Rule must always hold", "Move to a hook"]]),
  "Chat-fix-only", "You correct Claude in chat each time instead of editing the file, so every session repeats the mistake.", "Turn each repeated correction into a CLAUDE.md edit the same day.",
  "adding to memory is Module 3.3.", "repeat equals edit."),
 sec("Version control and review", "Commit the project file so teammates inherit it, and review CLAUDE.md edits in pull requests like any documentation change so conventions track the code [Claude Code docs, Large codebases, 2026]. Keep personal preferences in `CLAUDE.local.md` and add it to `.gitignore` [Claude Code docs, Best practices, 2026]. Block-level HTML comments are stripped before injection, so maintainer notes can live in the file without costing context [Claude Code docs, Memory, 2026].",
  "```markdown\n<!-- maintainer: keep under 200 lines; run /doctor before each release -->\n# Conventions\n- Prefix commit subjects with the package name\n```\n\n" + tbl(["Artifact", "Git"], [["`CLAUDE.md`", "Commit"], ["`CLAUDE.local.md`", "Gitignore"], ["`.claude/rules/*.md`", "Commit"]]),
  "Comment-in-code-block", "You put a maintainer note in a code block expecting it to be stripped, but comments inside code blocks are kept and cost context.", "Use a block-level HTML comment outside any code block.",
  "rules directory is Module 3.3.", "only block-level comments are stripped."),
 sec("Testing and revisiting", "Test changes by watching whether behaviour shifts. Revisit instructions after major model releases, since workarounds for an older limitation can become overhead, and consider a Stop hook that receives the session transcript path and proposes CLAUDE.md updates while the gap is fresh [Claude Code docs, Large codebases, 2026].",
  tbl(["Step", "Evidence"], [["Pick a task that exercises the line", "Task text"], ["Run before the change", "Result"], ["Change one line", "Diff"], ["Run after in a fresh session", "Result"]]),
  "Untested-edit", "You merge a CLAUDE.md change after reading it, never watching whether behaviour changed.", "Attach a before and after run of one relevant task to the pull request.",
  "evals are Module 10.1.", "no evidence, no merge."),
 ],
 [("Name three signals to edit CLAUDE.md.", "Claude repeats a mistake, review catches something it should have known, you retype the same correction."),
  ("Where do personal preferences go and what else must you do?", "`CLAUDE.local.md`, and add it to `.gitignore`."),
  ("You correct in chat every time. Name the failure mode by its exact label.", "**Chat-fix-only.**"),
  ("A maintainer note in a code block still costs context. Name the failure mode.", "**Comment-in-code-block.**"),
  ("You merge an unobserved change. Name the failure mode.", "**Untested-edit.**"),
  ("Which comments are stripped before injection?", "Block-level HTML comments, not those in code blocks."),
  ("What can a Stop hook do for CLAUDE.md upkeep?", "Receive the transcript path and propose updates."),
  ("When should you revisit the file besides on errors?", "After major model releases."),
  ("How many variables should a behaviour test change?", "One line per change.")],
 [("Pick a recurring correction", "Trigger"), ("Write one line to address it", "Diff"), ("Run a task before the change", "Before"), ("Run after, in a fresh session", "After"),
  ("Open a PR with both runs attached", "PR text"), ("Add a block-level maintainer comment", "Comment")],
 ["Run", "Before", "After"], [["Task exercising the line", "Mistake repeated", "Mistake gone"]],
 [["PR with no run evidence", "**Untested-edit**"], ["Note placed in a code block", "**Comment-in-code-block**"]],
 "The artifact passes only if both runs are attached and the change touched one line.",
 [["Anthropic", "Memory", "Triggers, comment stripping", "Current"], ["Anthropic", "Large codebases", "PR review, Stop hook upkeep", "Current"], ["Anthropic", "Extend Claude Code", "Update triggers", "Current"]],
 "none found between the sources read.",
 "This is the craft of upkeep: **hypothesis, diff, test, merge.** The engineer who understands this does not ask \"is the file good?\" — they ask \"what did this change do to behaviour?\""))

